[FOREIGN]

00:07:10.839 --> 00:07:14.676
[MUSIC]

00:07:14.676 --> 00:07:19.177
[FOREIGN]

00:07:19.177 --> 00:07:23.059
[MUSIC]

00:07:56.630 --> 00:08:07.624
[MUSIC]

00:08:07.624 --> 00:08:08.490
Is not incredible?

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:13.244
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I'm

00:08:13.244 --> 00:08:17.440
going to make four observations
about this signature story.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.340
First of all, it's really powerful.

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:21.510
It is intriguing.

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:24.970
Don't you wonder what Atari was doing?

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.470
Being so obsessed with the tree,
and taking care of the tree?

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:34.690
And then we get kind of a surprise.

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.860
We find out that that tree she marked,

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.740
when she knew she was
going to have a baby.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.800
And that tree has been
the symbol of the babies growth.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:47.370
And now the baby reached five.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.982
And isn't it authentic?

00:08:57.633 --> 00:08:59.210
We get to know Atari.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.530
We get to know her father.

00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:03.230
We even see the village they're living in.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.360
It is nothing, but real.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.760
It has to be authentic to us.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.840
And third it's involving, isn't it?

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.850
You get drawn in, you get emotionally
involved in the whole story.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:23.820
And you really care, so it's powerful.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:27.569
Second thing is that
it communicates facts.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:35.770
Now, we know from all kinds of research
that if you put facts out there.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:38.340
A list of four, or five,
or six, seven facts.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.914
Like 2 million children die
before the age of five.

00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:44.690
And the delight life

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.500
program that involves all kinds of things
in the school and out of the school.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:53.020
And that will affect these,
you put a bunch of facts out there.

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:58.751
People don't attend to them,
they don't notice them.

00:09:58.751 --> 00:10:03.740
Their brain kind of says, you know
here's three or four or five facts.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:05.970
Should I process them?

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.470
I'll probably forget them.

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.770
They probably won't have any use for me.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:12.770
Don't just ignore them.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:15.360
Facts get ignored.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:22.120
And the worst thing is even if they get
processed, even if people for one reason,

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:26.740
or another process these facts,
they don't persuade.

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:32.129
And there's several reasons for that, but
one of them is you have counter arguing.

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:37.840
When you see facts in the natural
inclination is to say,

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:44.780
this guy is trying to persuade me,
what is he not telling me?

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:48.750
Maybe 5 million children,
how does he know it's 5 million children?

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.430
Where does he get the data?

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.900
And how do you know that this washing
hands is going to affect that, and

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.870
how much it will affect that, and
maybe it'll just have a tiny effect.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:00.560
It's counter-arguing.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.750
So, now, think, inter-story.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:07.100
So, instead of printing the facts,
we tell a story.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:14.923
And what we can do is we can
slip the facts in to the story.

00:11:14.923 --> 00:11:19.910
So, when you hear the story you come to
know that there is a problem with infant

00:11:19.910 --> 00:11:20.770
mortality.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:25.490
And it's related to hand-washing.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:28.110
And so, they slip that in,
but even more important,

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:33.460
they set you up, so, now,
the facts are relevant.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:36.860
So, now, you're willing to process facts.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.090
And you remember them,
because they're attached to the story.

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:45.212
So, we have some facts that
are kind of within the story, but

00:11:45.212 --> 00:11:47.033
then we have some facts at the end.

00:11:47.033 --> 00:11:51.707
And the story motivates you
to process those facts.

00:11:51.707 --> 00:11:55.960
The third thing is,
that this video is incredibly successful.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:11:59.950
11 million people watched this video.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:04.110
And it cost nothing for
Lifebuoy to put it out there.

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.880
11 million people watched it.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.140
And not only that, but
there was two companion videos.

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.129
One of them had a girl named
Shaka that was seven years old.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:23.390
And she represented a unborn baby
projected forward seven years.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.880
So, she was sort of not a ghost, but

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:32.600
a projection commenting on her seven
years that hadn't occurred yet.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:33.400
But will occur.

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:40.290
And she was talking to her mother, and
she talked about how she sang lullabies,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:44.940
how she read stories, and
how she washed her hands.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:49.718
And the girl was so
grateful that her mother had done that.

00:12:51.369 --> 00:12:52.940
That got 14 million views.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.830
The three of them together
got 44 million views.

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.883
And that doesnt count all of
the sort of press results,

00:13:03.883 --> 00:13:08.148
or press accounts of this whole program,
and

00:13:08.148 --> 00:13:12.657
these signature stories
within this program.

00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:19.512
And it also created a nice feeling,

00:13:19.512 --> 00:13:24.720
you watch this video and
you feel good, don't you?

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.820
You feel good about the program help
a child reach five, you feel good about

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:33.810
Life Boy, there's some people that
would even feel good about Unilever.

00:13:33.810 --> 00:13:36.954
But most people don't know
Life Boy is made by Unilever.

00:13:36.954 --> 00:13:41.850
Unilever, incidentally, is the company
I admire more than any other,

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:44.490
because of their philosophy of business.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:45.320
It's quite remarkable.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:52.580
But that feeling in the video
gets transferred to the brand.

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:58.600
And we know that's to again,
hundreds of research studies.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:02.950
We know if you like an ad, you're going
to like the brand that did the ad.

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:03.886
It gets transferred.

00:14:03.886 --> 00:14:06.983
We know that.

00:14:06.983 --> 00:14:12.638
And it was successful in helping this
program help a child reach five,

00:14:12.638 --> 00:14:17.220
because Life Boy is one half

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:23.100
of their goal of getting a billion people
to change their hand washing habits.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:28.050
They're halfway there after eight years,
and this video helped them get there.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:32.649
Fourth thing I wanted to say is
that this is a signature story.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:39.470
And a signature story is intriguing,
gets attention.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:44.500
It is authentic, and
that means that words like phony,

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:49.850
selling, this is just a selling job, they
don't enter your mind if it's authentic.

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:54.660
It's involving,
that means you get drawn in and

00:14:54.660 --> 00:15:00.200
that's one of the most powerful
characteristics of any story.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:05.320
And it's a narrative, that means it's sort
of once upon a time this happened and

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:10.280
then that happened, and
it's not a set of facts.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:14.700
And it takes as a strategic message.

00:15:15.850 --> 00:15:18.850
In this case the strategic
message is about

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:23.230
explaining the infant mortality rate.

00:15:23.230 --> 00:15:28.840
The fact that the hand washing can
prevent that and there's a program.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.600
It's help a child reach five.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.258
And it's done by Life Boy.

00:15:37.865 --> 00:15:42.797
And I should say also it's
a strategic story for this program,

00:15:42.797 --> 00:15:45.680
Help a Child Reach Five.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:50.850
It's a strategic story for
Life Boy, the brand Life Boy.

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:55.250
And in some cases, for some people,
it's a signature story for Unilever.

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.530
If you look at the Unilever.

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:03.710
Any report you'll see
a reference to this story.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:07.940
So, why do we use signature
stories as perferms.

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:08.880
Let me give you two reasons.

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:14.080
First of all, again,
there's study after study after study,

00:16:14.080 --> 00:16:19.240
probably 500 studies,
that demonstrate vividly

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:24.900
that if you compare facts to a story,
it has the same general content.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:29.020
A story will get more attention.

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:33.650
It will be, it will persuade.

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:36.410
It will change perceptions.

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:37.840
It will be remembered.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.070
And it will be passed on.

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:45.090
Much more than facts.

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:48.106
And these, we're not talking
about a little bit of improve,

00:16:48.106 --> 00:16:52.056
we're talking about orders of magnitude,
two times, five times, ten times.

00:16:53.489 --> 00:16:56.175
And the second reason we're
using signature stories,

00:16:56.175 --> 00:16:58.840
come back to the digital
world we're living in.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:03.080
Marketers struggle how do we
cope with the digital world?

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:03.829
Things are different.

00:17:04.900 --> 00:17:08.380
My take on digital,
is that content is king.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.680
And content means stories.

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:18.737
So, company after company are learning
that it's good to actually hire

00:17:18.737 --> 00:17:23.697
journalists, have a little department and
create and manage stories.

00:17:26.299 --> 00:17:31.360
So let me now,
turn to introducing Jennifer.

00:17:33.420 --> 00:17:34.030
I have to say,

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:39.944
that I had mixed feelings about
having such an accomplished daughter.

00:17:39.944 --> 00:17:43.878
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; On one hand, I get some reflected glory

00:17:43.878 --> 00:17:48.448
Because people think I had
something to do with that.

00:17:48.448 --> 00:17:50.520
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Which is not true, but

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:53.460
it doesn't affect the fact I
still get reflected glory.

00:17:55.600 --> 00:18:01.020
But there's some issues, first of all,
people call me up regularly.

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.730
I mean, honest to God they call me up and
say, Jennifer's busy,

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:04.905
would you like to speak on?

00:18:04.905 --> 00:18:10.050
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:14.958
I used to be the primary speaker,
now I'm just kind of the warmup act.

00:18:14.958 --> 00:18:22.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And just literally two weeks ago,

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:27.480
my friend and colleague Scott Davis,
was at a conference call with me.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.860
And he said,
Dave I gotta tell you a story.

00:18:32.090 --> 00:18:34.660
My son Ben is in the business
school in Indiana,

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:39.490
and I said to him I know it's coming now,
I hear this all the time.

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:42.090
His Professor is using my book, or

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:45.100
his Professor is said something
about one of my ideas.

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:51.490
He said, his professor played
a five minute video on humor from

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:56.245
Jennifer Aaker, and it was terrific, and
isn't that the daughter of your friend?

00:18:56.245 --> 00:19:01.070
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And then,

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:04.300
in the same conversation,
Same conversation,

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:09.120
Scott said, well your new book on,
I'm writing a book on strategic stories.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.220
That's going to be my next book.

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:16.265
I expanded an article Jennifer and I did
for California Management into a book.

00:19:16.265 --> 00:19:20.990
And he said,
you gotta call it Aaker Strategic Stories.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:23.206
Because you got Aaker on brand in,

00:19:23.206 --> 00:19:27.750
Aaker strategies, Aaker on you can
have a whole bunch of books and

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:31.840
it would be a series, and
I thought that was a really great idea.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:35.560
But then I thought most
people are going to think

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:37.195
Jennifer finally wrote a second book.

00:19:37.195 --> 00:19:40.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.225
my wife suggested why don't you call
it Dee Aaker on Strategic Stories?

00:19:44.225 --> 00:19:50.008
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I

00:19:50.008 --> 00:19:53.320
tell you working with Jennifer
is not a walk in the park.

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:56.330
She is very opinionated.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:03.520
And we have these vigorous conversations
about issues we disagree on and

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.089
they always end one of three ways.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:09.080
She will send after fights
she'll get exhausted.

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:12.785
And she'll say,
dad you gotta stop being repetitive.

00:20:12.785 --> 00:20:15.230
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:18.375
she will say, dad you gotta
work on your listening skills.

00:20:18.375 --> 00:20:21.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But the worst is,

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:25.960
I'll be in a situation where I'll tee up,
Some, crystallized.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:30.370
Three reasons why her
position is just flat wrong.

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:32.420
And anybody could see that.

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:36.645
And just as I get into that,
she'll say, gotta go, love you.

00:20:36.645 --> 00:20:41.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I'm talking to dead air.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:45.480
Now she's a really amazing person,

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:50.450
she's literally opened
the five research areas for

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:56.240
her field like brand personality,
cross culture, research and

00:20:56.240 --> 00:21:00.780
humor and meaningfulness and
she's just a wonderful person,

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:07.924
she's the most caring empathetic person
I know, and she's my best friend.

00:21:07.924 --> 00:21:10.766
&gt;&gt; Aw.
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:10.766 --> 00:21:12.390
&gt;&gt; Thank you!

00:21:12.390 --> 00:21:16.581
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Go ahead and

00:21:17.812 --> 00:21:20.112
&gt;&gt; After my mom, Jan and Jolyn,

00:21:20.112 --> 00:21:24.070
after the three of you,
then I'm his best friend.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:29.500
I'm just saying that, so, thank you dad.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:34.390
It's totally true that my favorite way
to end a conversation with my dad is,

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:40.580
"Gotta go, I love you.", and so
I am really honored to be here today.

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:45.580
There are so many people int
he room that I recognize and

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:50.120
I really wanted to say thank
you not just to my dad,

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:54.216
who I think we all, can we al just say
like who does not love Dave Aaker.

00:21:54.216 --> 00:21:57.399
[Clapping].

00:21:57.399 --> 00:22:02.440
It's so funny like,
I saw a few of you before,

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:07.355
and you are talking about
how great Dave is and

00:22:07.355 --> 00:22:11.249
I'm like yeah I've got to go, ok.

00:22:11.249 --> 00:22:15.973
So you are so beloved and
have had such an impact its amazing,

00:22:15.973 --> 00:22:22.190
I also wanted to thank my sister Jan, and
Jolyn, Rich and Brian for being here.

00:22:23.730 --> 00:22:27.350
Our family means everything to us.

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:31.590
And so one of the incredible
honors of living here and

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:35.840
being here tonight is
really you guys being here.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:36.440
Where's my Mom?

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:39.570
All right.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:41.350
And our heart and soul of the family.

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:42.240
It's so nice to be here.

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:45.235
Anyway, I already saw Dad
looking at his watch so.

00:22:45.235 --> 00:22:48.673
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I wanted to start off this with

00:22:48.673 --> 00:22:51.605
the first [LAUGH] quote that I could,

00:22:51.605 --> 00:22:57.393
because I didn't have time to prep today,
I've been busy working hours.

00:22:57.393 --> 00:23:01.488
Dad's been thinking about this talk,
I just got out of class at 5:30,

00:23:01.488 --> 00:23:03.030
rushed over here.

00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.310
This morning, I'm like I'm going
to put together some thoughts.

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:06.490
He's like what are you going to do?

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:10.840
And he goes the worse case scenario,
the worst thing you can do is go along.

00:23:10.840 --> 00:23:12.360
And then he don't go too long.

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:17.600
That's all I ask, so and then he said
what are you going to talk about?

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:18.450
So I said well,

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:21.890
I was thinking about talking about how do
you create personal signature stories?

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:23.070
You're going to talk
about company stories.

00:23:23.070 --> 00:23:24.730
I'm going to talk about
personal signature stories.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:26.050
I think that's really interesting.

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:26.825
And he goes hm.

00:23:26.825 --> 00:23:28.910
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:31.330
it was pretty much nothing after that.

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:35.370
And if you know Dad, there's one of three
ways that he responds after the silence.

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:39.120
because he is incapable of
maintaining the silence.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:39.690
Have you?

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:41.670
No, not going to happen.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:46.550
So what are the three ways
that guy responds after he,

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:50.240
cannot stop, but
actually have to say something.

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:51.125
The first thing is.

00:23:51.125 --> 00:23:56.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And it's actually heard as?

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:02.660
Okay, and what that means is I
totally disagree with you but

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:09.680
I'm not going to say it out loud,
because then you will hang up on me.

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:11.880
So that's what it means.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:17.194
The second thing is, okay,
and it's heard as oookay.

00:24:17.194 --> 00:24:20.897
And what that means is,
I totally disagree with you but

00:24:20.897 --> 00:24:23.647
I really don't want you to hang up on me.

00:24:23.647 --> 00:24:26.340
But I really disagree with you.

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.610
And the third thing you'll say is, no.

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:31.710
And what it sounds like is, no, no,
no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no!

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:36.580
That's basically, it's a two year old,
no, you've got it all wrong.

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:39.195
And so that's basically
what I heard this morning.

00:24:39.195 --> 00:24:41.627
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All three of them.

00:24:41.627 --> 00:24:45.077
Wait a minute, and then he said,
I go, well [LAUGH] and

00:24:45.077 --> 00:24:47.016
then he offers suggestions.

00:24:47.016 --> 00:24:50.066
And he goes, what you really
should talk about is purpose,

00:24:50.066 --> 00:24:52.941
like you're killing it with
this whole purpose thing.

00:24:52.941 --> 00:24:57.646
And I go well I think I might talk about
how do you cultivate signature stories.

00:24:57.646 --> 00:25:00.123
He goes no, no, no, no, no.

00:25:00.123 --> 00:25:02.311
And so,
what I'm going to talk about today is,

00:25:02.311 --> 00:25:04.522
how do you create personal
signature stories?

00:25:04.522 --> 00:25:08.341
And we have 50 slides, so just buckle in.

00:25:08.341 --> 00:25:10.555
We're just going to get very comfortable.

00:25:10.555 --> 00:25:13.809
So how do you create
personal signature stories?

00:25:13.809 --> 00:25:19.010
The first is carve out clear
areas of incompetence.

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:24.343
By the way, these were all completely
inspired by our family and

00:25:24.343 --> 00:25:26.531
my father in particular.

00:25:26.531 --> 00:25:31.590
Be sneaky, seek highs and
lows, and bank your stories.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.312
It's fine if they are brief and pithy.

00:25:33.312 --> 00:25:34.980
So that's what I'm going
to talk about today.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:39.540
The first thing is carve out
clear areas of incompetence.

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:44.180
I think it's fair to say, Tommy, that
dad has done an extraordinarily good job

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:47.280
of carving out these clear
areas of incompetence.

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:49.322
Earlier days, he never took a picture.

00:25:49.322 --> 00:25:50.960
I don't think he's ever
actually taken a picture.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:54.902
This is my mom taking a picture
of me being held by my dad.

00:25:54.902 --> 00:25:59.263
He's very good at holding people,
he's good at picking clothing.

00:25:59.263 --> 00:26:03.397
He's not great at actually
taking a picture.

00:26:03.397 --> 00:26:06.520
Very clear in social settings,
not good at that.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:09.215
He's also not great at elegant eating.

00:26:09.215 --> 00:26:14.038
So if you are at a dinner party and
you might have a muffin over here,

00:26:14.038 --> 00:26:17.890
very good chance he might
have a nibble of it.

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:22.540
And also maybe like have some
disastrous unintended consequences.

00:26:22.540 --> 00:26:25.764
Now this sounds like I'm making fun
of dad, I'm not making fun of dad.

00:26:25.764 --> 00:26:28.644
He is a genius.

00:26:30.838 --> 00:26:32.974
He is a genius.

00:26:32.974 --> 00:26:35.539
My husband, Andy Smith, is he here?

00:26:35.539 --> 00:26:36.299
All right.

00:26:36.299 --> 00:26:38.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My husband, Andy Smith,

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:39.530
is also a genius.

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:43.130
I find a lot of men in my life
are geniuses in this way.

00:26:43.130 --> 00:26:47.300
So carving out clear areas in competence,
if I could just share a quick story.

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:50.600
So Andy, when we got married early on,

00:26:50.600 --> 00:26:54.840
I decided wasn't going to be that 70 year
old Grandma who took out the garbage.

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:56.280
I really wanted my husband to do that.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:59.770
And you know in the first week of
marriage, whoever takes out the garbage

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.965
first is basically going to take out
the garbage for the rest of their life.

00:27:02.965 --> 00:27:06.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I knew that, I'm no dummy.

00:27:06.517 --> 00:27:08.537
Got dad as my dad.

00:27:08.537 --> 00:27:09.942
And so I said,
would you take out the garbage?

00:27:09.942 --> 00:27:12.287
And he said yeah, absolutely I would.

00:27:12.287 --> 00:27:17.776
So when it didn't go out I thought
that was curious but I wasn't worried.

00:27:17.776 --> 00:27:24.940
I said, hm, probably used words
to describe what I wanted.

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:28.580
I'll put a calendar request
on his calendar and

00:27:28.580 --> 00:27:31.680
he will accept it and then it will go out.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:34.300
So when it didn't go out,
I thought that was weird.

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:37.327
So I updated the task request
on his Google Calendar.

00:27:37.327 --> 00:27:41.405
And I said garbage goes out, happy face,
happy face, exclamation point,

00:27:41.405 --> 00:27:45.102
exclamation point in case he was
conditioned as a young child to think

00:27:45.102 --> 00:27:47.480
that garbage going out
is a negative thing.

00:27:47.480 --> 00:27:52.690
I was going to classically condition
him to believe it was a positive thing.

00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:56.170
And so he accepted that update and
it didn't go out.

00:27:56.170 --> 00:28:01.464
And so I said what's going on,
why is the garbage not going out?

00:28:01.464 --> 00:28:04.100
I don't know how to be more clear.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:07.640
And he goes I would like
to take out the garbage but

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:09.992
I'm very confused,
what time does it go out?

00:28:09.992 --> 00:28:12.326
Does it go out on the right
hand side of the driveway or

00:28:12.326 --> 00:28:13.930
the left hand side of the driveway?

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:18.667
And after they pick it up,
when do I take it in or?

00:28:18.667 --> 00:28:20.087
I had take out the garbage.

00:28:20.087 --> 00:28:23.591
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Our kids take out the garbage.

00:28:23.591 --> 00:28:25.404
Other people take out the garbage now.

00:28:25.404 --> 00:28:26.770
But it's beautiful.

00:28:26.770 --> 00:28:30.760
Carving out clear areas of confidence
not only gives you stories but

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:34.490
it also reduces the amount of time
you actually have to do work.

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:39.100
So what I had done, by the way, once I
learned these techniques from other people

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:43.470
is I decided to actually carve
out incompetence around cooking.

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:48.430
So not only do I not cook, the few
times I do cook and I burn myself, I

00:28:48.430 --> 00:28:53.850
take a picture and post it on social media
so that people can be clear, I don't cook.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:55.817
This is what happens when I cook.

00:28:55.817 --> 00:29:00.170
All right the second,
be sneaky and then brand things.

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:05.680
In one study, we asked individuals
what's your happiest childhood memory?

00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:06.400
We asked parents.

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:07.920
We asked kids.

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:13.380
And these are the types of answers
that were revealed in the study.

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:17.489
Constructing Leaf Forests with my brother,

00:29:17.489 --> 00:29:22.269
our Family Read day the day after
Christmas, fishing for tadpoles with Jane

00:29:22.269 --> 00:29:26.749
in the summer, dancing to
Michael Jackson's Beat It with my friends,

00:29:26.749 --> 00:29:31.454
our Linda Evangelista Spring Cleaning
Fashion Shows, and Special Days,

00:29:31.454 --> 00:29:33.716
I said, actually, Special Days.

00:29:33.716 --> 00:29:36.340
So, let me tell you what special days are.

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:41.940
Special days are when dad took Jan or
Joe or I when we were young,

00:29:41.940 --> 00:29:46.178
and basically,
we were on a need to know basis.

00:29:46.178 --> 00:29:47.993
So, where are we going to go?

00:29:47.993 --> 00:29:49.551
He wasn't going to tell us.

00:29:49.551 --> 00:29:52.477
And so, but
he would want to do certain things.

00:29:52.477 --> 00:29:57.425
Like he'd want to go to the Cal game but
we wouldn't want to go,

00:29:57.425 --> 00:29:59.960
so he'd call it a special day.

00:29:59.960 --> 00:30:01.296
Wouldn't tell us where
we were going to go,

00:30:01.296 --> 00:30:02.523
we're just going to go on a special day.

00:30:02.523 --> 00:30:04.473
And we were so excited.

00:30:04.473 --> 00:30:06.086
My God, it's a special day.

00:30:06.086 --> 00:30:10.297
And then we would arrive at Cal Berkeley
and we'd have pompoms, get them there and

00:30:10.297 --> 00:30:12.670
cotton candy at the time and stuff.

00:30:12.670 --> 00:30:17.930
And it actually ended up being
ridiculously memorable, a fantastic story.

00:30:17.930 --> 00:30:21.910
And actually I'm here right
now talking about it.

00:30:21.910 --> 00:30:24.970
And so
he was sneaky in very systematic ways and

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:27.850
then he would brand them as special days.

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:29.520
I mean it is genius, right?

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:30.769
He probably took us on five.

00:30:30.769 --> 00:30:34.920
Mom, who did way more stuff with this,
and mom, just no offense,

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:39.935
did not brand those things as special days
and now we remember the special days.

00:30:39.935 --> 00:30:45.638
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Third, seek highs and lows.

00:30:45.638 --> 00:30:48.888
So when you think of personal signature
stories, you're really not thinking of

00:30:48.888 --> 00:30:52.756
I was winning, winning, winning, winning,
winning, won, got the promotion, whatever,

00:30:52.756 --> 00:30:54.790
you're really thinking about highs and
lows.

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:57.740
These are the opportunities for
stories, right?

00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:00.530
So when dad says intriguing,
authentic, involving.

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:04.251
Involving a big piece of that is feeling
like you're going on a high and a low, and

00:31:04.251 --> 00:31:06.420
so you're designing these roller coasters.

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:09.443
And that makes sense,
because when we see movies,

00:31:09.443 --> 00:31:12.267
like James Bond,
they all have highs and lows.

00:31:12.267 --> 00:31:13.889
The arc here is wow, what happened?

00:31:13.889 --> 00:31:15.485
I don't know, he gets the girl.

00:31:15.485 --> 00:31:16.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:31:16.470 --> 00:31:20.312
every single James Bond film has that arc.

00:31:20.312 --> 00:31:22.304
Or Jurassic Park, what's going on?

00:31:22.304 --> 00:31:23.402
T-Rex.

00:31:23.402 --> 00:31:23.990
What's going on?

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:24.790
Big T-Rex.

00:31:24.790 --> 00:31:26.851
What's going on?
Big, big T-Rex.

00:31:26.851 --> 00:31:29.136
So arcs don't have to be sophisticated,

00:31:29.136 --> 00:31:31.970
they just have to have some highs and
lows.

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:35.946
Or Casablanca, which has these,
sort of, micro story themes in it.

00:31:35.946 --> 00:31:39.436
And so when you are finding
signature stories for yourself,

00:31:39.436 --> 00:31:43.680
or your family, or your life,
you're looking for those highs and lows,

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:47.529
which is a really exciting way to
actually raise kids for example.

00:31:47.529 --> 00:31:51.470
because when the lows hit,
you know it's goinna be a great story.

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:54.980
And if you can have a comedic lens
That allows them to take risks and

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:57.480
you don't take things so seriously.

00:31:57.480 --> 00:31:59.940
It also allows you to document things.

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:03.720
So I wanted to mention that Disneyland is,
as I think we can all agree,

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:04.595
the worst place ever.

00:32:04.595 --> 00:32:07.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Very, very unhappy place.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:11.100
But most people don't remember that
because they don't take pictures of

00:32:11.100 --> 00:32:12.700
the unhappy moments.

00:32:12.700 --> 00:32:13.260
We do.

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:17.390
We do and
we also write it up in our holiday card.

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:20.380
I'll share one from a few years back.

00:32:20.380 --> 00:32:24.730
Cooper, one of our sons,
wrote a highlight of the year: Disneyland.

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:28.870
We prepared for the trip for weeks,
filling our jean pockets with cookies for

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:32.860
sustenance and
brushing up on our Disneyland history.

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:36.930
On arrival though,
we found that the parking was painful,

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:39.020
tickets expensive, lines long.

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:42.640
On the upside, my dad found
an iPhone app with a GPS driven map,

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.710
ranked attraction checklist, and
crowdsourced wait-time guide

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:50.560
to get us around the park strategically,
optimizing our fun while minimizing lines.

00:32:50.560 --> 00:32:53.110
Unfortunately the iPhone
died in 30 minutes.

00:32:53.110 --> 00:32:56.140
Necessity forced us to try
something arguably better.

00:32:56.140 --> 00:32:59.350
A cleverly, color coded conveniently
foldable pocket size tool.

00:32:59.350 --> 00:33:00.480
It's called a map.

00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:02.450
It's provided by Disneyland.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:03.600
It's amazingly free.

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:05.645
The only free thing at Disneyland.

00:33:05.645 --> 00:33:08.110
[LAUGH] So that was fortuitous.

00:33:08.110 --> 00:33:11.240
On the way out, we met Sandra (our
au pair) and Tea Sloane (our sister)

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:14.610
outside the Haunted House,
where Sandra was shaking from heatstroke.

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:17.385
Being a "doctor," my mom
recommended she eat some grapes.

00:33:17.385 --> 00:33:21.115
[LAUGH] Seconds later, Sandra's body
decided to put the grapes in reverse.

00:33:21.115 --> 00:33:25.310
[LAUGH] We left with Sandra to find
a place with less bad advice and

00:33:25.310 --> 00:33:26.740
fewer bystanders.

00:33:26.740 --> 00:33:29.250
She slept the entire car ride home, and

00:33:29.250 --> 00:33:30.580
we were required to be silent for
the whole time.

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.690
So it ended up on an upnote.

00:33:32.690 --> 00:33:36.025
We can't wait to go back.

00:33:36.025 --> 00:33:37.060
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So now the question is,

00:33:37.060 --> 00:33:38.950
why would you seek highs and lows?

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:41.710
I know Disneyland's awful.

00:33:41.710 --> 00:33:48.120
Why would I go with my family, besides,
they're my family and you should go?

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:52.980
What's actually experienced is,
you get there to Disneyland, and

00:33:52.980 --> 00:33:55.770
then you get in lines and
you don't realize how long they are.

00:33:55.770 --> 00:34:01.070
And then you get to Small World which its
branded The Happiest Place On Earth, true?

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:01.680
I'm not sure.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:06.010
But then you take a picture
of Devon literally crying,

00:34:06.010 --> 00:34:07.750
which I always find hilarious.

00:34:07.750 --> 00:34:09.010
And then they're tired.

00:34:09.010 --> 00:34:12.580
Cooper and Devon are twins,
are on Andy's lap, fatigued.

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:14.830
But then you get the fireworks,

00:34:14.830 --> 00:34:18.470
and then you forget the cotton
candy all over their face.

00:34:18.470 --> 00:34:21.400
And Tea's is a mess, and
our au pair's throwing up.

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:23.900
But you just remember the end, right?

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:26.858
You remember the peak, and
you remember the end, and

00:34:26.858 --> 00:34:29.510
that has disproportionate impact.

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:32.310
So, if we seek highs and
lows, and if we design for

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:36.730
memories rather than just experience,
we'll cultivate a life of story.

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:42.160
And last, bank your stories,
even if they're short and pithy.

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:44.270
We talk a lot about sort
of six word stories, and

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.780
I find this to be a really powerful tool.

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:51.060
Larry Smith and others have actually
generalized this concept, but

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:55.770
it was really born by Hemingway
who once wrote baby shoes.

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:56.300
For sale.

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:57.060
Never Worn.

00:34:57.060 --> 00:35:01.980
So I made my students in the Power of
Story class write six-word stories to

00:35:01.980 --> 00:35:07.580
describe their lives, and
you all shared stories.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.690
So, and
we're going to share a few of these.

00:35:10.690 --> 00:35:16.080
Stories that you,
I'll just like have you look at them.

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:20.720
See if we can have a little
vote on which one's best.

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:22.260
Dad, which one did you say you liked?

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:23.478
There was a few that you really liked.

00:35:25.146 --> 00:35:26.366
&gt;&gt; I like the MBA theater.

00:35:26.366 --> 00:35:28.200
&gt;&gt; The MBA theater.

00:35:28.200 --> 00:35:30.030
&gt;&gt; Get lucky once, twice.

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:31.256
&gt;&gt; Get lucky once, twice?

00:35:31.256 --> 00:35:36.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and ambition with purpose.

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:38.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah, Robert Chatwani who's here but
had a huge impact.

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:42.650
Just huge impact on my life for
the two years I was at Haas.

00:35:42.650 --> 00:35:45.240
He led to The Drive and Fly Effect,
was a book that Andy and

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:48.090
I wrote together with Carly Adler.

00:35:48.090 --> 00:35:50.056
I also liked,
married the wrong girl, fixed it.

00:35:50.056 --> 00:35:57.710
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But the best was Kay Aaker's, so Kay,

00:35:57.710 --> 00:36:03.700
you get the award for best six word story.

00:36:03.700 --> 00:36:05.360
We got here, thank you Rich.

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:09.470
And for the others,
we're going to have three runners up,

00:36:09.470 --> 00:36:13.355
which we will tell you about later,
and you will get some books.

00:36:13.355 --> 00:36:16.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Branding.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:18.900
These six word stories
are really important.

00:36:18.900 --> 00:36:20.430
You can do them in the day.

00:36:20.430 --> 00:36:22.990
You can have your kids do them at night.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:27.330
Instead of having a conversation about
what you did today over the dinner table,

00:36:27.330 --> 00:36:30.810
you could have a conversation
about what story was unlocked.

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:34.380
And how many of you have soulless
conversations at the dinner table about

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:35.080
what did you do today?

00:36:36.800 --> 00:36:37.930
And then,
what are you going to do tomorrow?

00:36:37.930 --> 00:36:39.960
Like that was one thing.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:44.720
You were always, Dad, so
averse to us talking about logistics.

00:36:44.720 --> 00:36:45.810
But that was defines our life.

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:47.540
It's to-do lists oftentimes.

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:48.480
It's logistics.

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.700
It's goals that we think
are really important.

00:36:50.700 --> 00:36:54.030
But what actually defines
our lives are the stories.

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:55.380
And if you think of.

00:36:55.380 --> 00:36:59.800
Your wedding if you had one, or
potentially someday you might die,

00:36:59.800 --> 00:37:02.350
and the memorial you might have.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:03.740
What are the stories that are shared?

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:07.280
It's not a set of lists, it's not
a set of things that you accomplished.

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:11.940
It's the stories that
others remember about you.

00:37:11.940 --> 00:37:15.220
So bank the stories,
even if it's six words or less.

00:37:16.360 --> 00:37:19.440
And bank those stories that are distinct,
that make you or

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:21.910
your family truly distinct.

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:28.710
Bold, and so you take risks that
are relevant to different audiences.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.710
Dad talked a lot about authenticity and
truth.

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:36.070
We're living in a time right now where
there's a significant trust deficit.

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:38.760
We don't trust necessarily
our politicians.

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:40.710
We don't trust oftentimes leaders.

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:43.280
And it's not just the finance industry.

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:44.520
It's everywhere.

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:49.180
But what people trust is people they know,
people whose story they believe in.

00:37:49.180 --> 00:37:53.000
And so cultivating those stories
becomes really, really important and

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.700
codifying them and capturing them.

00:37:55.700 --> 00:37:56.250
Does as well.

00:37:57.490 --> 00:38:01.730
These bold stories, these memorable
stories so that they stick over time.

00:38:01.730 --> 00:38:06.350
Stories that are sharable so
that they can spread and garner action,

00:38:06.350 --> 00:38:09.900
and stories that depict something larger,
something meaningful.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:14.650
So I just wanted to end with
one of my favorite quotes.

00:38:14.650 --> 00:38:18.496
Tell me the true facts and I'll learn,
tell me the truth and I'll believe.

00:38:18.496 --> 00:38:22.335
But tell me a story and
it will live in my heart forever.

00:38:22.335 --> 00:38:23.318
Thank you.

00:38:23.318 --> 00:38:31.763
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:38:31.763 --> 00:38:33.738
&gt;&gt; By the way, there's oops!

00:38:33.738 --> 00:38:36.812
We have some questions, alright,

00:38:36.812 --> 00:38:39.540
should I sit here
&gt;&gt; Why don't you sit here and David,

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:42.000
if you would come up as well.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:45.540
You submitted questions and
thank you for that in advance,

00:38:45.540 --> 00:38:49.480
I'll just kick this off but I know,
I think you have some other questions.

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:51.910
The first question which I think
is a little bit personal but

00:38:51.910 --> 00:38:55.305
I'll start with it, I know a lot of
you had this on your mind Jennifer and

00:38:55.305 --> 00:38:59.340
David would you tell us
your signature stories,

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:06.020
please, each of you.

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:06.520
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:07.900
&gt;&gt; You want me to start?

00:39:07.900 --> 00:39:09.925
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Well in I was teaching strategy at

00:39:09.925 --> 00:39:10.500
Berkeley, and

00:39:10.500 --> 00:39:13.930
I had written a book on strategy
&gt;&gt; And

00:39:13.930 --> 00:39:19.500
I came to realize that people were
too focused on short-term profits.

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:26.283
Enduring by the stock market and others,
not to blame the Rich for that but...

00:39:26.283 --> 00:39:29.211
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He was one of them.

00:39:29.211 --> 00:39:35.144
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He was part of the evil empire.

00:39:35.144 --> 00:39:39.890
Gee I'm tempted to tell.

00:39:39.890 --> 00:39:44.620
When I was side by side with him I
used to go over and say, you know

00:39:44.620 --> 00:39:48.498
if I spent so much time with students I
could win the best teacher award too.

00:39:48.498 --> 00:39:53.025
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But anyway.

00:39:53.025 --> 00:39:56.600
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I said, how can I do this,

00:39:56.600 --> 00:39:57.840
how can I influence this?

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:02.770
And I decided I wanted to spend the rest
of my career teaching people to manage for

00:40:02.770 --> 00:40:03.440
the long term.

00:40:03.440 --> 00:40:09.830
Which to me meant to build assets instead
of driving short-term financials.

00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:14.110
And so I said,
what kind of assets should I do?

00:40:14.110 --> 00:40:20.990
And given my background in market
research and strategy and in advertising.

00:40:20.990 --> 00:40:26.080
And also, I realized that brand equity

00:40:26.080 --> 00:40:31.070
was starting to become
a topic of conversation.

00:40:31.070 --> 00:40:32.910
And I thought it should be brands.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:34.460
That's where I fit.

00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:38.500
And I looked at other kinds
of assets of a firm and

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:40.090
I wasn't very relevant to any of them.

00:40:40.090 --> 00:40:44.770
So I decided to help people build brands

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:50.170
as a way to focus on assets
instead of financials.

00:40:50.170 --> 00:40:52.322
And so I kind of grandiosely said,

00:40:52.322 --> 00:40:56.310
I'm going to try to change the way
people think of marketing and

00:40:56.310 --> 00:41:00.510
the way they think of brands and
the way they run their business.

00:41:00.510 --> 00:41:06.020
I would be embarrassed to say that then,
but now I'm older, who cares.

00:41:08.710 --> 00:41:11.200
So anyway that's how I
started in branding.

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:14.200
I wrote my first book sort
of defining brand equity and

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:17.820
my second book how do
you manage brand equity.

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:21.440
And since then I continue
to write branding books and

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:25.340
be really focused on that basic problem.

00:41:25.340 --> 00:41:28.224
How to get people to
think more strategically.

00:41:31.222 --> 00:41:36.865
&gt;&gt; That's fantastic, I would say my
equivalent of that one would be,

00:41:36.865 --> 00:41:39.744
I never wanted to be in marketing.

00:41:39.744 --> 00:41:41.744
So I-
&gt;&gt; So

00:41:41.744 --> 00:41:43.620
you're implying you
[INAUDIBLE] in marketing?

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:47.220
&gt;&gt; No, I'm still not, I'm even
less in marketing than I ever was.

00:41:47.220 --> 00:41:50.810
But my goal was to always
be a great hopefully.

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:56.130
A great mom,
hopefully a great partner, Andy,

00:41:56.130 --> 00:41:59.567
this is where you say like yes, nailed it.

00:41:59.567 --> 00:42:05.370
[LAUGH]
And I wanted to be a great friend and

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:08.950
hopefully an athlete and hopefully
[INAUDIBLE] about something in the world.

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:14.090
And wanted to do great in my career,
but I just didn't know what it was.

00:42:14.090 --> 00:42:16.140
And I think,
when you talk to executives or

00:42:16.140 --> 00:42:20.680
anyone, you found out that a lot
of people have closet careers.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:24.030
They wanted to be this, but
they didn't know how or whatever.

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:27.140
And so for me that closet
career was to be an oncologist.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:32.590
My mom's father,
my grandfather died of cancer early age.

00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:37.060
She's worked for
hospice now for gosh, 40 years,

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:42.910
meals on wheels with others in the room.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:46.500
And it just had a personal impact even
though I didn't see it personally.

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:49.011
But I think all of us have
been touched by cancer.

00:42:49.011 --> 00:42:53.149
And so
that was what was really important to me.

00:42:53.149 --> 00:42:56.282
But I couldn't see my way
to being an oncologist, but

00:42:56.282 --> 00:42:58.270
also having these other goals.

00:42:58.270 --> 00:43:01.237
To be all of these other things.

00:43:01.237 --> 00:43:05.767
And so
I saw my dad be this remarkable dad.

00:43:05.767 --> 00:43:13.190
Who was so good with time management and
so fantastic with ideas and science.

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:15.090
And I thought it was so

00:43:15.090 --> 00:43:18.720
inspirational, so that's why I
became a behavioral scientist.

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:26.230
But every Sunday,
I would feel a little bit depressed.

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:31.181
I don't know how many of you guys
on Sundays or like one day a week,

00:43:31.181 --> 00:43:33.623
you just kind of get depressed.

00:43:33.623 --> 00:43:39.877
And ten years of depression,
on Sundays, I saw it coming, it hit it,

00:43:39.877 --> 00:43:45.923
it was consistent and I thought,
is it that I have a lack of friends.

00:43:45.923 --> 00:43:49.251
What is it?

00:43:49.251 --> 00:43:51.691
But it would have something
to do with meaning for me,

00:43:51.691 --> 00:43:54.960
I just felt not very connected to
something that was truly meaningful.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:01.340
So fast forward,
I got recruited back to Stanford.

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:05.550
I was lucky enough to join Berkeley for
two years and thank you for

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:08.150
those of you who took my class and
are here.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:09.780
You mean a lot to me.

00:44:09.780 --> 00:44:14.678
I have a deep deep love for
Berkley and Bears, but

00:44:14.678 --> 00:44:17.677
then I went back to Stanford.

00:44:17.677 --> 00:44:23.280
But I do have a feeling
of what's right for me.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:27.170
And when I came back to Stanford,
which felt very right for me.

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:32.434
At the time I had the opportunity
to basically really resonate with

00:44:32.434 --> 00:44:38.360
a story that Robert [INAUDIBLE] MBA shared
with me on the last day of my class.

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:40.920
Which is called creativity and innovation.

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:46.100
And in this story he told,
I told about his best friend Sumeir,

00:44:46.100 --> 00:44:50.570
many of you guys know this story,
who had leukemia.

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:55.800
And Robert because he's so strategic,
so creative, so confident with attitude.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:58.470
He was able to come up with a framework,

00:44:58.470 --> 00:45:02.430
a way of thinking about harnessing
story and social media.

00:45:02.430 --> 00:45:07.290
To get over 25,000 on the Bone Marrow
Registry in order to find a perfect match

00:45:07.290 --> 00:45:11.650
for Samir and he did that, and it was
just such a mesmerizing story for me.

00:45:11.650 --> 00:45:14.630
That Andy and I,
I went back that night and

00:45:14.630 --> 00:45:18.420
actually shared with him the story and
we ended up writing a book based on it.

00:45:18.420 --> 00:45:19.400
I never wanted to write a book.

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:21.290
That was dad's thing.

00:45:21.290 --> 00:45:26.805
But it literally felt compelled to me,
and I could go on,

00:45:26.805 --> 00:45:30.925
on about the reverberating
effects of that signature story.

00:45:30.925 --> 00:45:33.859
But even though I never
became an oncologist,

00:45:33.859 --> 00:45:38.515
I felt donating a year of my life to try
and get more people on the bone marrow

00:45:38.515 --> 00:45:40.895
registry and
still now really feeling connected.

00:45:40.895 --> 00:45:45.760
That very meaningful thing A,
I no longer have empty Sundays.

00:45:45.760 --> 00:45:51.580
I feel very full on Sundays, and B, we
have this book which is why you wrote so

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:55.690
many of your books that it creates legacy.

00:45:55.690 --> 00:45:59.540
It creates meaning, and so that's one
of my signature stories and I have so

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:00.250
many more too.

00:46:00.250 --> 00:46:04.830
But hopefully you'll all be thinking
of in every domain of your life,

00:46:04.830 --> 00:46:07.530
one signature story that
really defines you.

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:10.130
And if you haven't yet,
please do so tonight.

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:13.410
It would make us incredibly happy
just even writing it down with

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:14.450
six words or less.

00:46:14.450 --> 00:46:17.764
Just a title and
then sharing it with your loved ones.

00:46:17.764 --> 00:46:19.010
That would be a big successful night.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:25.640
All right, so I'm going to jump in and
ask a couple questions.

00:46:25.640 --> 00:46:30.570
All right, so let's see, how do you create

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:35.050
a powerful personal story when
you're working in an ordinary job?

00:46:35.050 --> 00:46:39.540
I don't know if Marissa's here, but
Marissa asked that question that I loved.

00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:41.030
So I'm going to ask it to you.

00:46:42.920 --> 00:46:46.170
&gt;&gt; I don't think this is how it works,
we both answer these questions but anyway.

00:46:46.170 --> 00:46:48.410
&gt;&gt; Well, I will,
if I have something interesting to say.

00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:52.210
&gt;&gt; Anyway, well I think that if

00:46:52.210 --> 00:46:58.060
you're in a position in an organization
to create a higher purpose.

00:46:58.060 --> 00:47:02.891
Or to maybe activate a higher
purpose that is in the organization

00:47:02.891 --> 00:47:07.548
by creating a program or something,
then you should do that.

00:47:07.548 --> 00:47:11.549
I think that it just provides

00:47:11.549 --> 00:47:16.724
meanings in the lives of employees.

00:47:16.724 --> 00:47:22.180
And it provides respect for the people
do business with your organization.

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:26.490
But if you're not in a position to
do that, you might consider having

00:47:27.550 --> 00:47:32.400
another life, another professional life as
a volunteer perhaps with an organization

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:35.360
that does have a higher purpose
that you really admire.

00:47:36.940 --> 00:47:38.490
&gt;&gt; I totally disagree.

00:47:38.490 --> 00:47:39.000
I'm just kidding.

00:47:39.000 --> 00:47:41.380
I think that was a great answer.

00:47:41.380 --> 00:47:45.610
I see why you put that first,
because you wanted to talk about purpose.

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:47.330
Basically, you were going to fit that in.

00:47:47.330 --> 00:47:49.890
&gt;&gt; I was just trying to help you
out because purpose is kind of your

00:47:49.890 --> 00:47:50.528
thing now, isn't it?

00:47:50.528 --> 00:47:51.571
Yeah [LAUGH].

00:47:51.571 --> 00:47:54.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; After carving out clear

00:47:54.400 --> 00:47:56.720
areas of incompetence,
it will be my thing.

00:47:57.870 --> 00:48:02.776
It's true,
I teach a class with Emily Ma at Google X,

00:48:02.776 --> 00:48:06.277
and it is called rethinking purpose.

00:48:06.277 --> 00:48:11.435
The other thing I would add to that
answer is that a lot of times, in fact

00:48:11.435 --> 00:48:17.394
there is data by Gallop and others, that
show that at least in the United States.

00:48:17.394 --> 00:48:21.954
Over 50% of the people
working at their institutions

00:48:21.954 --> 00:48:26.418
don't understand the purpose
of their institution or

00:48:26.418 --> 00:48:31.100
their mission and
of those that do, 80% don't care.

00:48:31.100 --> 00:48:35.150
I mean, it's just huge data sets and

00:48:35.150 --> 00:48:40.639
it's a starkly depressing trend.

00:48:40.639 --> 00:48:43.310
So what happens if you're
at this ordinary job and

00:48:43.310 --> 00:48:45.370
there's no sort of higher purpose there?

00:48:45.370 --> 00:48:50.110
One interesting thing about
the purpose research is that

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:54.490
purpose oftentimes is thought as this
like purpose with a big capital P but

00:48:54.490 --> 00:48:57.280
it actually is a lower case p oftentimes.

00:48:57.280 --> 00:49:00.690
So everyone thinks it’s sort of this
destination, this inspired mission I

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:05.400
wanted to be out on colleges, make
a dent in cancer but more often than not

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:10.160
purpose is designed much more from
this lower case p perspective.

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:13.280
It’s what you're doing in the day to day.

00:49:13.280 --> 00:49:14.180
Are you learning?

00:49:14.180 --> 00:49:17.817
Do you feel like you're
accessing some unique strengths?

00:49:17.817 --> 00:49:20.630
Are you passionate about
what you're doing?

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:23.100
And do you feel like what you're
doing is what the world needs?

00:49:24.120 --> 00:49:26.710
And those are the elements
that get you into flow.

00:49:27.750 --> 00:49:30.330
That and whether you really like.

00:49:30.330 --> 00:49:35.160
The people you are working with at least
have one best friend at your organization.

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:39.420
So if you can just kind of get
into that sort of lowercase

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:43.030
key mentality that will
often write you out.

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:44.820
Okay, I'm going to have another question.

00:49:44.820 --> 00:49:47.950
What advice do you have for
individuals in organizations faced

00:49:47.950 --> 00:49:51.206
with a rival who generates powerful
stories that are patently untrue.

00:49:51.206 --> 00:49:52.728
Steve's question.

00:49:54.313 --> 00:49:59.047
&gt;&gt; Well, I'm a great believer
that the best story wins and

00:49:59.047 --> 00:50:01.670
the last heard story wins.

00:50:01.670 --> 00:50:04.250
So you've gotta really
change the conversation.

00:50:04.250 --> 00:50:09.350
I think you need to create
your own signature story, and

00:50:09.350 --> 00:50:13.850
make that more prominent
than this one that is,

00:50:13.850 --> 00:50:18.020
if you look at Walmart for example.

00:50:18.020 --> 00:50:20.990
There was a time when 90% of
the world wouldn't shop at

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:24.660
Walmart because they were so
disgusted with a lot of their policies.

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:31.280
And then Walmart, created this alternative
conversation they sort of went green.

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:34.400
They got religion about
environmental things.

00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:37.530
They're doing enormous stuff on that.

00:50:37.530 --> 00:50:39.991
They're working with a community and

00:50:39.991 --> 00:50:45.680
they've really provided at least
an alternate conversation.

00:50:45.680 --> 00:50:47.590
At least when you talk about Walmart,

00:50:47.590 --> 00:50:51.150
you don't have to talk about how
they treat their employees and

00:50:51.150 --> 00:50:55.040
how they buy from China and
how they treat their suppliers and so on.

00:50:55.040 --> 00:50:56.370
There's an option.

00:50:56.370 --> 00:51:02.420
So I think you have to create
a competitive, strategic story or

00:51:02.420 --> 00:51:08.959
signature story that wins the day
against the one that's false.

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:12.966
&gt;&gt; Totally disagree, I'm just kidding.

00:51:12.966 --> 00:51:17.523
Another way to think about it is listening
better to what that story is and

00:51:17.523 --> 00:51:22.007
the language that's being used by
that competitor, that audience and

00:51:22.007 --> 00:51:24.996
I've been thinking a lot
about this right now,

00:51:24.996 --> 00:51:28.830
it feels like we're living in
a relatively decisive world.

00:51:29.900 --> 00:51:31.970
One of the many reasons we're so

00:51:31.970 --> 00:51:37.094
happy to be here today is not just
because of our deep love for Rich and

00:51:37.094 --> 00:51:42.590
also what Haas has been
able to I think create for

00:51:42.590 --> 00:51:45.870
you and also for
us more generally, but also.

00:51:47.620 --> 00:51:52.920
This ability to bridge Stanford and
Berkeley, it's a very sort of small

00:51:52.920 --> 00:51:58.280
divisive, kind of funny,
competitive environment but

00:51:58.280 --> 00:52:02.940
it's still very gratifying to be able
to make these sort of small dents.

00:52:02.940 --> 00:52:07.520
When you're in divisive environments where
there's it's not clear what's truth and

00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:13.640
what's not, when there's different
people in organizations and groups

00:52:13.640 --> 00:52:18.970
that have alternative narratives, that
are very stuck in their head all of us.

00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:23.000
What I find to be interesting and
useful, and

00:52:23.000 --> 00:52:26.760
it's backed by a significant
amount of social science as well.

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:29.930
Is understanding and feeling empathy for

00:52:29.930 --> 00:52:34.230
individuals who have a very different
narrative, even if it's patently false.

00:52:35.450 --> 00:52:40.650
And understanding the language that
they're using, where they're coming

00:52:40.650 --> 00:52:46.550
from and so before you potentially
design a counternarrative.

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:51.130
And so understanding, having empathy and
understanding language and

00:52:51.130 --> 00:52:55.630
then aligning where there are some
common thread feels to be,

00:52:55.630 --> 00:52:59.530
to me, quite inspiring and hopeful.

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:02.169
&gt;&gt; Do we have time for
another question Arch?

00:53:02.169 --> 00:53:02.957
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:53:05.262 --> 00:53:13.210
I'm [LAUGH] just going to All
right going with James Tinsley.

00:53:13.210 --> 00:53:16.140
How have signature stories
changed in recent years?

00:53:16.140 --> 00:53:18.240
Are we telling the same stories
through different media or

00:53:18.240 --> 00:53:20.810
are we telling fundamentally
different stories?

00:53:22.380 --> 00:53:23.480
I can go if you don't.

00:53:23.480 --> 00:53:25.032
&gt;&gt; You go ahead.
&gt;&gt; Okay [LAUGH].

00:53:25.032 --> 00:53:27.400
I thought this was such
an interesting question and

00:53:27.400 --> 00:53:33.440
the reason is because we're living
in such a trust deficit and

00:53:33.440 --> 00:53:38.670
that stories and people that you trust
is so important, authenticity is at

00:53:38.670 --> 00:53:44.060
such a premium and I know its an overused,
kind of trendy word in many regards.

00:53:44.060 --> 00:53:46.930
But there's something important about

00:53:46.930 --> 00:53:51.600
stories that not only are authentic but
they feel authentic and

00:53:51.600 --> 00:53:54.680
I think that is really a significant
trend that's happening.

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:57.727
It's not really a qualitative
different type of story per se but

00:53:57.727 --> 00:54:01.398
it has implications, it's always going
to be pretty, pretty, pretty most

00:54:01.398 --> 00:54:04.852
marketing or manufactured goods or
it is going to be some highs and lows?

00:54:04.852 --> 00:54:09.558
Look at the story that dad shared,
it wasn't like happy happy happy happy,

00:54:09.558 --> 00:54:10.350
right.

00:54:10.350 --> 00:54:12.920
There were some highs and there was lows.

00:54:12.920 --> 00:54:17.590
Second shift is that I think
people need to design for

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:21.925
partners more, that you need to
feel like you're a part of a story.

00:54:21.925 --> 00:54:23.775
So it's not just like,
I'm going to share with you a story and

00:54:23.775 --> 00:54:25.225
you're going to receive it.

00:54:25.225 --> 00:54:27.735
But if you feel part of the story.

00:54:27.735 --> 00:54:30.465
This is what Dragonfly and
Robert story was all about.

00:54:30.465 --> 00:54:34.685
If you feel part of it,
then you'll get people aligned.

00:54:34.685 --> 00:54:37.505
And if you get people aligned,
and they feel part of the story,

00:54:37.505 --> 00:54:40.840
you can actually make action happen.

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:45.620
And I think we're all living in a world
right now where it's very important for

00:54:45.620 --> 00:54:52.330
us to be aligned at what is good in
the world, and how we can make action

00:54:52.330 --> 00:54:57.890
toward that what we believe in and
how we can make progress happen together.

00:54:57.890 --> 00:55:01.920
There is a phrase that I'll just
end with which is people want to be

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:04.930
valued members of a winning
team on an inspired mission.

00:55:04.930 --> 00:55:07.670
And you don't have to be
winning all the time.

00:55:07.670 --> 00:55:09.520
But have you heard this before?

00:55:09.520 --> 00:55:12.880
But you need to have
a pathway toward winning and

00:55:12.880 --> 00:55:16.320
I like that sentence because you have to
have clarity and that inspired mission.

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:19.690
You have to have people knowing
what role on the team do they have.

00:55:19.690 --> 00:55:24.830
&gt;&gt; I think another really important
thing in the branding world is energy.

00:55:24.830 --> 00:55:28.340
And we know that brands
have been declining for

00:55:28.340 --> 00:55:33.020
15 years all around the world, the
exception are those brands with energy.

00:55:33.020 --> 00:55:35.730
And it's really hard to
get energy into a brand.

00:55:35.730 --> 00:55:38.995
And one of the reasons,
a practical reason we

00:55:38.995 --> 00:55:42.790
want to higher purpose is because
they will give energy to the brand.

00:55:42.790 --> 00:55:45.690
You look at Lifebuoy,
I mean they make a bar of soap.

00:55:45.690 --> 00:55:50.480
It is pretty hard to create energy for
a bar of soap, but

00:55:50.480 --> 00:55:56.690
helping a child reach five and three
videos that got 44 million exposures?

00:55:56.690 --> 00:55:57.466
That's energy.

00:55:57.466 --> 00:56:04.574
And it gives life to one of the great
global brands which is Life Court.

00:56:06.180 --> 00:56:08.720
Fantastic, we have one last question.

00:56:11.181 --> 00:56:16.850
How do you create a six word story or
any signature story?

00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:19.700
This is Linda.

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:21.520
&gt;&gt; I have nothing to say about that.

00:56:21.520 --> 00:56:23.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, all right.

00:56:23.260 --> 00:56:26.200
One thing, I don't know if Linda's here,

00:56:26.200 --> 00:56:30.290
but what I would say is you're always
thinking about change or transformation.

00:56:30.290 --> 00:56:32.030
So think about your life.

00:56:32.030 --> 00:56:35.150
What did it used to be and
what are you now?

00:56:35.150 --> 00:56:36.620
So how did you sort of change.

00:56:36.620 --> 00:56:39.560
And that usually gets you away
from I am this, I'm going to this,

00:56:39.560 --> 00:56:43.030
I'm going to do this, et cetera,
that kind of linear progression.

00:56:43.030 --> 00:56:46.480
As you start to figure out
signature stories, baby shoes for

00:56:46.480 --> 00:56:48.910
sale number one there's an art to it.

00:56:48.910 --> 00:56:51.700
And there's kind of a reveal to it.

00:56:51.700 --> 00:56:54.920
And so think of transformation and change.

00:56:54.920 --> 00:56:58.710
And I think right now again,
I think all of us really

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:04.640
are in a stronger place
if we're adoptable, or

00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:07.530
open to change, and
we're able to transform.

00:57:07.530 --> 00:57:08.928
So that would be my one tip.

00:57:08.928 --> 00:57:11.862
Thank you.

00:57:11.862 --> 00:57:18.970
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Please

00:57:18.970 --> 00:57:23.470
join me in thanking these two wonderful
friends and scholars of all of us.

00:57:23.470 --> 00:57:33.145
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:57:33.145 --> 00:57:35.750
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] Thank you both.

00:57:35.750 --> 00:57:41.120
You know I think it was maybe a year and
a half ago that I saw Jennifer and

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:44.630
this idea came up, and we thought if
we could only get you two together.

00:57:44.630 --> 00:57:48.250
And I'm so
happy we were able to make this happen.

00:57:48.250 --> 00:57:52.650
When I was, when I was seven,
my brothers were 14 and 15, and

00:57:52.650 --> 00:57:58.480
one of the things I remember so distinctly
is my parents driving us to Half Moon Bay.

00:57:58.480 --> 00:58:02.700
We had one of those woody Station Wagons
and we filled it with pumpkins,

00:58:02.700 --> 00:58:07.340
it was early October, and we brought
those pumpkins back to our home and

00:58:07.340 --> 00:58:10.380
our parents let us sell those pumpkins.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:12.683
We had a stand in front
of the neighborhood and

00:58:12.683 --> 00:58:16.870
we sold those pumpkins and that was
a thrill for me, that was a thrill for me.

00:58:16.870 --> 00:58:20.560
And two years later I may
have been about nine and

00:58:20.560 --> 00:58:24.690
my brothers were, you know, the end of
high school we graduated to jewelry.

00:58:24.690 --> 00:58:27.375
They were in the puka shell business.

00:58:27.375 --> 00:58:30.490
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; High volume, low margin.

00:58:32.140 --> 00:58:34.680
I was seven years younger,
I didn't like the business.

00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:39.375
I was doing tiger eye, turquoise,
much high quality stuff.

00:58:39.375 --> 00:58:42.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, but I loved it.

00:58:42.390 --> 00:58:44.990
I absolutely loved that I got to
got to Berkley as an undergrad.

00:58:44.990 --> 00:58:49.160
And I come form a business family if you
hadn't noticed from those stories and

00:58:49.160 --> 00:58:51.910
I wanted to go into business and
I knew it thrilled me.

00:58:51.910 --> 00:58:52.990
I was a sophomore and

00:58:52.990 --> 00:58:56.620
I'd taken Intermediate Macroeconomics
at Berkeley, and

00:58:56.620 --> 00:58:59.850
one of my faculty members, the teacher,
I'd never talked to her one on one.

00:58:59.850 --> 00:59:02.600
She come sup to me after
handing back the midterm,

00:59:02.600 --> 00:59:04.320
and I I had done pretty
well on the midterm.

00:59:04.320 --> 00:59:06.760
And she said, come see me,
please, in my office.

00:59:06.760 --> 00:59:07.640
I went to go see her.

00:59:07.640 --> 00:59:10.950
Her name was Fran Ban Liu.

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:13.540
And she said, have you ever thought
about getting a PhD in economics?

00:59:15.871 --> 00:59:19.230
Neither of my parents
had a four-year degree.

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:22.580
That was not an identity I'd
ever even thought about.

00:59:23.930 --> 00:59:28.730
And I walked out of her office
never having thought about that.

00:59:29.870 --> 00:59:33.120
Couple weeks later she got me together
with somebody named George Akerlof.

00:59:33.120 --> 00:59:34.880
I didn't know who George Akerlof was.

00:59:34.880 --> 00:59:39.440
He subsequently won a Nobel Prize, but
she wanted me to sort of talk to somebody

00:59:39.440 --> 00:59:43.760
who had done that, and
we can't be what we can't see, so

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:49.540
when people ask me why does this
institution mean so much to you?

00:59:49.540 --> 00:59:51.850
You know that's my story.

00:59:51.850 --> 00:59:54.010
And I think a lot of us
have a story like that.

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:57.840
Whether your story is connect to
Stanford or Berkley or you know,

00:59:57.840 --> 01:00:01.520
these institutions matter and
it's why we're so invested in them.

01:00:01.520 --> 01:00:03.990
That's why we come together around them.

01:00:03.990 --> 01:00:06.010
And that's part of why
we keep them strong.

01:00:06.010 --> 01:00:07.500
And we must keep them strong.

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:11.480
So whichever of those two
institutions you're part of,

01:00:11.480 --> 01:00:15.380
keep leaning into them because
they just matter so much.

01:00:15.380 --> 01:00:16.600
Thanks for coming out tonight.

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:19.879
Go Bears.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

01:00:19.879 --> 01:00:21.110
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

01:00:21.110 --> 01:00:23.990
I'd like to thank Rick Splines and
the Halls Alumni Group for

01:00:23.990 --> 01:00:27.870
putting this event on and
inviting our friends from Stanford.

01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:31.690
And I hope you got a chance to
think about signature stories and

01:00:31.690 --> 01:00:33.850
apply them in your organization.

01:00:33.850 --> 01:00:35.520
&gt;&gt; Right, so
think about signature stories.

01:00:35.520 --> 01:00:39.300
Both at work and in life and even in the
day to day, when you're having your next

01:00:39.300 --> 01:00:44.160
dinner meal with your family, I think
about actually sharing a story rather

01:00:44.160 --> 01:00:47.480
than necessarily sharing what you did
today or what you're going to do tomorrow.

01:00:47.480 --> 01:00:50.850
I'm not saying you have to cook,
I'm just saying.

01:00:50.850 --> 01:00:51.560
&gt;&gt; Of course not.

01:00:51.560 --> 01:00:54.190
&gt;&gt; Ask people what their story is.

01:00:54.190 --> 01:00:56.184
Thank you so much.

01:00:56.184 --> 01:00:59.364
[MUSIC]

